  "answer_text": "You are likely looking for **Iuka, Mississippi**. \n\nBecause of sans-serif fonts, the capital letter **\u201cI\u201d** (as in India) is often mistaken for a lowercase **\u201cl\u201d** (as in lima), leading many people to search for \"Luka\" instead of \"Iuka\". \n\nPronounced **\"eye-YOO-kuh\"** (/a\u026a\u02c8ju\u02d0k\u0259/), Iuka is a historic and scenic city in the far northeast corner of Mississippi. It serves as the county seat of **Tishomingo County**. \n\n### 1. Geography and Nature\nUnlike the flat, low-lying landscapes associated with much of Mississippi, Iuka is nestled in the foothills of the Appalachian Mountains, offering rolling hills and unique topography.\n* **Woodall Mountain:** Located just south of Iuka, Woodall Mountain is the **highest point in Mississippi** at 806 feet (246 meters) above sea level. \n* **State Parks:** Iuka is very close to two major outdoor destinations:\n  * **J.P. Coleman State Park:** Located along the Tennessee River and Pickwick Lake, it is a popular spot for boating, fishing, and water sports.\n  * **Tishomingo State Park:** Renowned for its unique massive sandstone rock formations, hiking trails, and its position along the historic Natchez Trace Parkway.\n\n### 2. History\n* **Founding:** The town was founded in 1857 along the Memphis and Charleston Railroad and was named after Chickasaw Chief *Ish-ta-ki-yu-ka-tubbe* (known locally as Chief Iuka).\n* **The Civil War:** Iuka was the site of the **Battle of Iuka** on September 19, 1862. During this fierce clash, Union Major General William Rosecrans stopped the advance of Confederate Major General Sterling Price. General Ulysses S. Grant also used a local home (the Brinkley House) as his temporary headquarters during the war.\n* **\"The Marriage Capital of the South\":** In the late 19th and early 20th centuries, Iuka\u2019s local courthouse became famous as a wedding destination. Mississippi did not require a three-day waiting period, blood tests, or strict age verification, prompting thousands of couples from surrounding states like Alabama and Tennessee to travel to Iuka to quickly tie the knot.\n\n### 3. Quirky and Unique Attractions\n* **The Apron Museum:** Iuka is home to the only museum in the United States (and likely the world) dedicated entirely to the history, art, and fashion of aprons. It features thousands of unique aprons dating from the Civil War to the modern era.\n* **Mineral Springs Park:** In the late 1800s, Iuka was a popular health resort town. Visitors flocked there to drink from the local mineral springs, which were believed to have healing properties. Today, the park remains a peaceful place to walk and see the historic springs.",
